# The k8s half of the FIRST tenant of the cross-tenant security fixture: the
# namespace plus the one WorkerPool both tenants run on. The second tenant
# deploys tenant-namespace.yaml.tmpl instead, which brings a namespace and no
# pool of its own — see the package comment in suites/security.
#
# replicas is 1 deliberately. An ateom hosts one actor at a time
# (internal/ateomcapacity: actorsPerAteom = 1), so two tenants never run
# together on a worker; the boundary this fixture exercises is worker REUSE
# across time. Worker pools are not atespace-scoped (the scheduler filters on
# sandbox class, the label selector and capacity, never on the actor's
# atespace), so a single worker forces the second tenant to reuse the exact
# ateom the first vacated. That reuse is the whole point: it makes the attacker
# inherit the host on which the victim just left its per-actor state, which is
# the arrangement the isolation assertions have to hold under. On more than one
# worker the attacker may boot on a fresh ateom the victim never touched, and
# the host-state assertions weaken to a tautology.
#
# The workload label is a literal rather than ${FIXTURE_SUFFIX}-suffixed
# because both tenants' templates must select this one pool and they render
# with different suffixes. The two sandbox-class lanes run sequentially and
# each tears its pool down, and sandbox class is itself a scheduling
# constraint, so the shared label cannot cross a gVisor actor onto a micro-VM
# worker.

apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: ate-e2e${FIXTURE_SUFFIX}

---

apiVersion: ate.dev/v1alpha1
kind: WorkerPool
metadata:
  name: tenant
  namespace: ate-e2e${FIXTURE_SUFFIX}
  labels:
    workload: ate-e2e-security
spec:
  replicas: 1
  workerImage: ${ATEOM_IMAGE}
${WORKERPOOL_RUNTIME}
